# For walking robot
We are using: JointTrajectoryPoint
The idea here is to make the robot walks in the simulation without falling and mimicking OTTO walk&roll walk.
So how to achieve that ?
First of all, I will a tool in the simulation which allows me to control the robot.
By doing some research I found multiple ways of control. But the mains controller found interesting at this of the project are programmic control via a node, telop control via keyboard / teleoperation (remote ctrl) and manual control using rqt.

I begun with the manual ctrl (rqt). Since I don't know how to make the robot walk like wanted, I started manual to understand how the ros2 joints fonction by using joint_trajectory_controller. I tried to perform the walking robot manually while logging each state and undertand how the robot should perform each step. By doing that, I found the principle loop to perform a step. This manual approach allowed me to find the working sequence for the robot to walk: ean → pivot → center → lean other way → pivot → center.

Then I moved on programmic ctrl, I create a node and write the logic in a continuous loop to verify if the robot is able to walk on long distance, by performing the right steps and not falling.

| | Previous code | This version |
|---|---|---|
| Gait phases | 4 (tilt+swing combined) | 6 (lean → pivot → center × 2) |
| Hip role assumed | fore/aft swing | lateral lean ✓ |
| Foot role assumed | lateral tilt | forward pivot ✓ |
| "Down" / replant step | missing | explicit, faster timing |
| Phase timing | uniform `SPEED` | lean=0.8s, center=0.4s |
| Foot state during lean | reset to 0 | explicitly zeroed before next pivot |

By using that approach, I faced some issues, the robot was difting, walking in circle. And it was due to the fact that LEAN_INNER and LEAN_OUTER are not balanced.
I also faces some frictions issues: 
# Phase 5: Pivot right foot — but right_hip is STILL at -LEAN_INNER!
([-LEAN_OUTER, 0.0, -LEAN_INNER, +PIVOT], ...)
The right foot was planted and loaded (body weight on it from the lean) and I was trying to twist it while the hip is actively pressing down on it with -LEAN_INNER. The friction force in Gazebo was resisting the twist.

